  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 214 PM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nHeading into Friday, the departing warm front will advect warmer, \nmoister air into central and eastern Nebraska. This should allow for \nwarmer conditions Friday afternoon with guidance suggesting 850\nmb temperatures will be 8 to 12 degrees celsius, with southwest\nNebraska reaching upwards of 15 degrees celsius. This could \ntranslate to surface highs Friday being in to upper 50s to 60s \nacross the region. Southwest Nebraska in particular could reach \nup to 70 degrees, with NBM probabilities of > 70F being 40 - 60 \npercent. The daily high for Imperial that day is 75 degrees so \nthere still is some space to climb before potentially breaking \nat record. As a surface low in Colorado moves northeastward \nahead of an incoming upper trough moving across the Rockies, a \ncold front will develop in the Panhandle and move eastward \nacross Nebraska late Friday afternoon and evening. As this \nfront moves eastward, it could provide enough lift for light \nrain showers to develop across northeast to central Nebraska \nlate that evening. Accumulations however will be very little if \nany rain does fall. Behind this front will be drier air that \nwill push humidity to the low 30s across the Panhandle and \nsouthwest Nebraska. This will create some elevated fire weather \nconditions, but luckily there is minimal overlap with gusty \nwinds out east. \n\nFrom the departing cold front and upper trough moving into the \nNorthern Plains, weekend and early next week highs will fall \ninto the upper 30s to 40s across the area both Saturday and \nSunday. These temperatures will catapult our area 5 to 10 \ndegrees below seasonal norms for this time of year. \n\nThere is a signal early next week for some active weather. Both the \nEuro and GFS have shown a signal for a strengthening upper trough \nmoving across western CONUS and the exit regions of a jet embedded \nin the trough ejecting across northwestern KS and southwest NE. This \ncould bring snow accumulations with NBM probabilities giving a near \n30 to 40 percent chance of an inch of snow across southwest \nNebraska. While there is some uncertainty if this signal holds in \nadditional forecast packages, it should be monitored closely in case \nof any Thanksgiving travel impacts.",
